A decoder that drives a powered lower-limb exoskeleton is fitted once and must
keep working for weeks, while electrode impedance, montage and skin contact
change the second-order statistics that every spatial filter depends on.
Re-estimating the input covariance at inference and whitening by it, without
labels, corrects this shift inside the network and removes the need for a
calibration session. Such a layer carries a failure mode its offline
counterpart cannot have. A running estimate tracks whatever is currently
streaming, and under a protocol that presents classes in long contiguous blocks
it converges to the active class and whitens away the signal it was inserted to
protect.

We present a 24{,}181-parameter decoder built around such a layer, and derive an
admissible band for its adaptation rate from two timescales of the recording
protocol. On a seven-participant exoskeleton cohort recorded across weeks it
reaches $0.901$ balanced accuracy with expected calibration error $0.039$ ($0.868 \pm 0.026$ over three seeds under the trace-normalised estimator), at $53\,\%$ of the parameters of the strongest baseline in the same pipeline. The
layer reduces session-to-session covariance distance by $52.0 \pm 4.5\,\%$ and
$83.9 \pm 3.9\,\%$ on two independent cohorts, in 15 of 15 participants
($p \le 10^{-4}$), against a control with no systematic reduction. On the second
cohort, whose class blocks last 309 windows, the decoder transfers at $0.792$
without the layer, and enabling the layer costs $0.211$. Slowing adaptation
past the block length recovers $0.090$ to $0.098$ on every arm that uses
alignment and exactly $0.000$ on the arms that do not, while the same change
costs $0.060$ on the first cohort, so the two optima are opposite.

The band then predicted a third cohort in advance. With class blocks of five
windows, both predictions committed before any model was trained held: enabling
alignment changed accuracy by $+0.002$, and slowing it by $-0.006$, where the
same slowing had recovered $0.090$ on the second cohort. Retraining after ICA
artefact removal lowers accuracy by $0.051$ and leaves it far above chance, and
the losses do not follow the amount of muscle activity removed. A trained
selective head lowers accuracy in 14 of 18 arms and is reported as a negative
result. Both bounds of the band are properties of the experimental design, so
the condition can be checked before a model is trained.
